The wind on the Isle of Solitude did not blow; it screamed. It was a constant, high-pitched wail that had long since worn the granite of the lighthouse base into smooth, bone-like curves. For Elias Thorne, the sound was as familiar as his own heartbeat. For forty years, he had been the keeper of the light, a solitary sentinel against the dark, watching the same gray sea churn against the same jagged rocks.

Elias was a man of few words and fewer visitors. The supply boat came once a month, dropping off canned goods, coal, and newspapers that were already stale by the time they arrived. He lived in a world of routine: winding the clock, polishing the brass, checking the wick, and sleeping in the small room at the top of the spiral stairs.

The letter arrived on a Tuesday, tucked under the door of the supply shed. It was not from the mainland authorities, nor from the lighthouse commission. It was a simple, cream-colored envelope, postmarked from a city three hundred miles away, written in a hand that was elegant but hurried.

